# Architecture Guardrails

Construction Runner: field workers (mobile) and office managers (web). Prefer matching existing patterns over introducing new ones.

| Layer | Location | Stack |
|-------|----------|--------|
| Mobile | `sitehub_worker_Ready/` | Flutter (Dart), device APIs |
| Web | `sitehub-admin/` | Next.js, TypeScript, Tailwind |
| Backend | `supabase/` | PostgreSQL, RLS, Realtime, Storage |

## Flutter (`sitehub_worker_Ready/`)

- Match the existing state-management approach on new screens—**never mix** architectures.
- Keep widgets modular; extract shared UI when reused.
- Camera, GPS, and offline paths: handle async errors clearly; cache where the codebase already does for poor connectivity.

## Next.js (`sitehub-admin/`)

- Follow existing App Router / client-boundary patterns in nearby files; do not refactor working pages to Server Components unless asked.
- Use Tailwind and existing component patterns already used on the page you edit.

## Supabase

- Web: prefer `@supabase/ssr` helpers already used in the project. Mobile: `supabase_flutter`.
- Client / browser code must not use the service role. Server API routes may use the service role when authorization is enforced in code (existing admin pattern).
- Prefer typed models/interfaces aligned to the schema over untyped JSON maps for domain data.

## Assistant output

- Production-ready, modular code—no `// TODO: implement later` placeholders.
- Do not redesign polished screens or invent extra UI unless the user asks.
